For this fixed workload, baseline/original p95 medians were about 4.64×/4.73× the percentile candidate's median, with separated observed ranges. Its large-standard median regressed approximately 0.20% against baseline and 3.82% against original; all-99/all-124 medians regressed **10.22%/4.45% against baseline**. Those control ranges overlap. Its complete standard and shared-special bodies are byte-identical to baseline; source identity alone does not establish why the timing controls differ.

## Isolated memory observations

Each memory child loaded exactly one reducer. Original children legitimately loaded released registry/lodash dependencies; native children loaded their single selected ESM reducer and common test helper. A separate authenticated original-only preflight generated five complete expected graphs shared across both manifests. Measured children read only selected source hashes/small manifest/helper, not full archives or another reducer. Actual owned-PID observer startup/handshake succeeded. Sampling was 100 ms; some short children had only one/two observer samples, so sampled maxima may miss brief peaks. Worker checkpoints and inclusive lifetime high-water RSS are separate retained views.

The maximum independent-observer RSS was 184,811,520 bytes in the standard sequence and 185,757,696 bytes in the percentile sequence, below the unchanged 512 MiB limit. Both used a 256 MiB old-space limit and 120-second per-child bound. Old-space is not total RSS. JSON/CSV preserve cold initialized/input/warm snapshots, sixteen transient block tails, retained/released snapshots, their within-child differences and all ranges. RSS/heap/external/arrayBuffers are bytes; lifetime maxRSS is KiB, not a resettable allocation counter.

In the percentile trial, p95 sampled transient RSS median was 89,288,704 bytes for candidate versus 91,529,216 for baseline. Retained-versus-beforeRetention heap-used medians were 90,184 versus 97,672 bytes, while released-versus-beforeRetention medians were 21,392 versus 28,880. The **within-child retained-minus-released heap median was exactly 68,792 bytes for both**. This does not support a lower retained-result-object-footprint claim. The common validation/GC context contributes to the other deltas.

Release-time RSS often increased after serializer validation despite heap reclamation. The report preserves **123 standard and 133 percentile negative paired sample deltas**, including 16/18 groups with negative medians; none are clipped or treated as negative allocation totals. For example, percentile original all-124 released heap delta ranged from −11,320 to 14,824 bytes. Returned/cache serialization happens after retained observation and before release/GC, and its temporary heap-capacity/RSS pressure may persist. These are diagnostic process observations, not allocator totals, leak proof or guaranteed lower memory. Fixed shared inputs and small percentile-retention counts limit generalization. Memory observations do not override unresolved timing regressions.

## Source and evidence identity

The frozen readiness receipt remains `dispatch-validation.json`, SHA-256 `6ef6d6ef1f79159391469df09b527485fecb62b9284b11da9a6ff888832525e3`; all 167 inventoried files were verified unchanged. Candidate/baseline/actual released original/archive/lodash and harness identities remain bound in the report. Exact measurement sources and original runtime/archive bytes are separately preserved in `preserved/measurement-sources/`. The percentile candidate's earlier full semantic/held-out/work-cap receipts retain their original execution paths and were not rebound. Production and both older stages remain unchanged.

Actual original authority is `@grafana/data@13.2.3` released archive `954a03a68fa122ebe74c0c318dc801e434c17294c5374ef593cf491f73c81c41`, with actual lodash archive/runtime/resolution authentication. Native baseline is `9269cf8c32be9bf2c075b67019e82e41272d9495f0321fefc17b2c5695a91371`; standard candidate is `e30ab8a663a22b43f384ec1de689175d81bdc7e1076b81fc5cca6083964e9e29`; separate percentile candidate is `6ac1153c008d960de23766af1e4325cc0e77a5ab10c49be604d4d6e203f6e26f`. Node executable/version/V8/platform/architecture and complete source fingerprints are in JSON. Plotting used separate post-window Matplotlib 3.10.8 libraries in ignored `.plot-libs`; application runtime dependencies were unchanged.

One post-window summarizer attempt reused small-workload checkpoint names across cases and failed. Its source and exact stdout/stderr are preserved under `preserved/consolidation-attempts/` and `results/consolidation-attempt-1791837710944.*`; only the new unfrozen summarizer was corrected, then it passed as `consolidation-attempt-1791837721347.*`. This was not a measured reducer/observer failure. Earlier rejected/confounded optimization trials and disclosures remain untouched in the prior frozen stage.

## Prospective next experiment

First isolate shared-control overhead of the percentile selector: a separate compact module-boundary/dispatch experiment can retain baseline standard and multi-selection bodies byte-for-byte while isolating the single-selector code footprint. Source layout/inlining is a hypothesis, not an established cause of the current regressions; module-loading overhead must remain an explicit cold-footprint control. Preserve ordinary primitive coercions, signed-zero/nonfinite/sparse stable-sort fallbacks, partition work cap and caller/cache aliases. Re-run full/held-out/adversarial acceptance before another newly coordinated measurement interval. Independently shaped p95 inputs and existing shared-calculation controls are required. Do not combine this with the standard candidate or infer that a profile establishes speed. No new candidate, workload or integration is authorized by this recommendation.

All exhaustive-feature, full-product and universal-superiority gates remain false. No integration/publication occurred in this stage.
